  <diagram id="ZVygQKpZJGKEOQZHik3p" name="Page-10">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1030" dy="628" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="850" pageHeight="1100" math="0" shadow="0">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />
        <UserObject label="&#xa;1. &quot;Do you want to replace our existing database?&quot;&#xa;No. It sits beside it and keeps what the database throws away. The database&#39;s job is to be current; the archive&#39;s is to remember what was. In fact the database is one of the things being archived — what was its state two years ago is a question it has no facility for.&#xa;&#xa;2. &quot;Why add another layer to our stack?&quot;&#xa;You already have that layer — a wiki page, a chat thread, a file server, a retention policy. It just isn&#39;t a system, so it decays silently. This is one binary, a config file and storage you already have. It isn&#39;t on the request path.&#xa;&#xa;3. &quot;Why not just use Postgres? Why not Neo4j?&quot;&#xa;We do — as substrates. The store interface is get/put/has, so anything that keeps bytes qualifies, and a Neo4j layer contributes query capability on top of durability. What no relational or property store gives you, however you model it: a record that proves itself to a third party, merge of independent replicas without a resolver, and derivation rather than versions.&#xa;&#xa;4. &quot;We have backups.&quot;&#xa;Backups guard the bytes; this guards readability and findability. A two-year-old dump needs the infrastructure of two years ago; a plaintext readout needs nothing. Keep both — the dump can live in the archive as content, tethered to the readout.&#xa;&#xa;5. &quot;We have Splunk / Datadog / ELK.&quot;&#xa;They answer the question this week, at a price per gigabyte that forces retention in days or months. This answers it in year two. Same question, different time horizon.&#xa;&#xa;6. &quot;Can you delete from an immutable archive? DSGVO requires it.&quot;&#xa;Yes. The bytes go, a documented gap stays — either an expiry the claim carried from the start, or a later claim requesting removal. The deletion itself is recorded, which is more than a conventional delete leaves behind.&#xa;&#xa;7. &quot;How would another database fix our bad habits?&quot;&#xa;It doesn&#39;t. Systems file, not people — the triage tool, the pipeline, the release job deposit what they already produce. And the real failure isn&#39;t discipline: material you did file is gone because retention purged it or the platform moved. It doesn&#39;t fix bad habits; it stops good ones from evaporating.&#xa;&#xa;8. &quot;Isn&#39;t this event sourcing, or an audit log?&quot;&#xa;Those capture temporal history — when facts changed. This captures epistemic history — how knowledge was derived from sources through chains of processing.&#xa;&#xa;9. &quot;Isn&#39;t this RDF / PROV / nanopublications?&quot;&#xa;PROV-DM is a vocabulary, conceptually compatible, not a storage structure. Nanopublications are a flat set of immutable assertions with no derivation DAG and no semantic layer.&#xa;&#xa;10. &quot;You said human-readable, but the claims are CBOR.&quot;&#xa;Envelope and payload are different questions. The id is a signature over a hash of the serialised record, so the envelope must be canonical — one record, exactly one byte string. The content is a MIME type, and the recommendation there is open text.&#xa;&#xa;11. &quot;A two-year-old SQL dump is useless without the infrastructure.&quot;&#xa;It&#39;s text. An agent can read the DDL and the inserts and pull the rows you asked about, with no engine and no migration chain. And the sequence of dumps shows how the schema evolved — including when something disappeared. You never wanted the database back; you wanted one row.&#xa;&#xa;12. &quot;Won&#39;t daily snapshots explode in size?&quot;&#xa;Identical bytes are one blob, so snapshot per table or per entity and everything unchanged costs nothing. Beyond that: an initial full plus readable diffs, with periodic fulls to bound the replay.&#xa;&#xa;13. &quot;Can you prove the archive is complete?&quot;&#xa;No archive on earth can — an archive contains what was put in it. Which is why the adoption work is process design: making the deposit a side effect of steps that already happen.&#xa;&#xa;-----&#xa;&#xa;That&#39;s Paper 2&#39;s two axes exactly — in time, an immutable, application-independent form that outlives the tool; in breadth, the scattered silos drawn into one searchable store where each fact carries its history. Worth borrowing that pair for the motivation slide; it&#39;s two sentences and it covers everything you&#39;ve been circling.&#xa;&#xa;The word doing the work is web, and it&#39;s worth distinguishing from co-location, because a sceptic will hear &quot;so, a shared drive with a search box&quot;. A shared drive gets you documents that happen to sit together; you still have to know what to search for, and you find the CVE record but not the triage note, not the test run, not the release it went into. What makes it a web is that the items cite each other: search finds one thread, and provenance walks you to the rest of the neighbourhood — including the pieces you didn&#39;t know existed, which in the court-case scenario are exactly the ones that matter.&#xa;&#xa;So the pairing for the slide is something like: everything is filed; nothing is findable. Search gets you a document. The edges get you the case." link="1. &quot;Do you want to replace our existing database?&quot;No. It sits beside it and keeps what the database throws away. The database&#39;s job is to be current; the archive&#39;s is to remember what was. In fact the database is one of the things being archived — what was its state two years ago is a question it has no facility for.2. &quot;Why add another layer to our stack?&quot;You already have that layer — a wiki page, a chat thread, a file server, a retention policy. It just isn&#39;t a system, so it decays silently. This is one binary, a config file and storage you already have. It isn&#39;t on the request path.3. &quot;Why not just use Postgres? Why not Neo4j?&quot;We do — as substrates. The store interface is get/put/has, so anything that keeps bytes qualifies, and a Neo4j layer contributes query capability on top of durability. What no relational or property store gives you, however you model it: a record that proves itself to a third party, merge of independent replicas without a resolver, and derivation rather than versions.4. &quot;We have backups.&quot;Backups guard the bytes; this guards readability and findability. A two-year-old dump needs the infrastructure of two years ago; a plaintext readout needs nothing. Keep both — the dump can live in the archive as content, tethered to the readout.5. &quot;We have Splunk / Datadog / ELK.&quot;They answer the question this week, at a price per gigabyte that forces retention in days or months. This answers it in year two. Same question, different time horizon.6. &quot;Can you delete from an immutable archive? DSGVO requires it.&quot;Yes. The bytes go, a documented gap stays — either an expiry the claim carried from the start, or a later claim requesting removal. The deletion itself is recorded, which is more than a conventional delete leaves behind.7. &quot;How would another database fix our bad habits?&quot;It doesn&#39;t. Systems file, not people — the triage tool, the pipeline, the release job deposit what they already produce. And the real failure isn&#39;t discipline: material you did file is gone because retention purged it or the platform moved. It doesn&#39;t fix bad habits; it stops good ones from evaporating.8. &quot;Isn&#39;t this event sourcing, or an audit log?&quot;Those capture temporal history — when facts changed. This captures epistemic history — how knowledge was derived from sources through chains of processing.9. &quot;Isn&#39;t this RDF / PROV / nanopublications?&quot;PROV-DM is a vocabulary, conceptually compatible, not a storage structure. Nanopublications are a flat set of immutable assertions with no derivation DAG and no semantic layer.10. &quot;You said human-readable, but the claims are CBOR.&quot;Envelope and payload are different questions. The id is a signature over a hash of the serialised record, so the envelope must be canonical — one record, exactly one byte string. The content is a MIME type, and the recommendation there is open text.11. &quot;A two-year-old SQL dump is useless without the infrastructure.&quot;It&#39;s text. An agent can read the DDL and the inserts and pull the rows you asked about, with no engine and no migration chain. And the sequence of dumps shows how the schema evolved — including when something disappeared. You never wanted the database back; you wanted one row.12. &quot;Won&#39;t daily snapshots explode in size?&quot;Identical bytes are one blob, so snapshot per table or per entity and everything unchanged costs nothing. Beyond that: an initial full plus readable diffs, with periodic fulls to bound the replay.13. &quot;Can you prove the archive is complete?&quot;No archive on earth can — an archive contains what was put in it. Which is why the adoption work is process design: making the deposit a side effect of steps that already happen.✻ Baked for 36s❯ Yes, most things get filed already...but nobody finds anything because it&#39;s too many silos... so ranke builds a web of all those documents, also over time, and makes it searchableThat&#39;s Paper 2&#39;s two axes exactly — in time, an immutable, application-independent form that outlives the tool; in breadth, the scattered silos drawn into one searchable store where each fact carries its history. Worth borrowing that pair for the motivation slide; it&#39;s two sentences and it covers everything you&#39;ve been circling.The word doing the work is web, and it&#39;s worth distinguishing from co-location, because a sceptic will hear &quot;so, a shared drive with a search box&quot;. A shared drive gets you documents that happen to sit together; you still have to know what to search for, and you find the CVE record but not the triage note, not the test run, not the release it went into. What makes it a web is that the items cite each other: search finds one thread, and provenance walks you to the rest of the neighbourhood — including the pieces you didn&#39;t know existed, which in the court-case scenario are exactly the ones that matter.So the pairing for the slide is something like: everything is filed; nothing is findable. Search gets you a document. The edges get you the case." id="UcU1BU8XV1e9DSAewbGm-1">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="text;whiteSpace=wrap;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontSize=14;fontColor=default;labelBackgroundColor=default;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="640" width="560" x="70" y="80" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
